1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,373
Dana's version of this document is one page, taped above her monitor.

2
00:00:04,373 --> 00:00:08,047
It earns that spot because it doesn't stay a one-time exercise.

3
00:00:08,047 --> 00:00:10,845
It's what she actually checks new ideas against.

4
00:00:10,845 --> 00:00:14,577
Time to build the whole thing, for a real product, not Harlan's.

5
00:00:14,577 --> 00:00:19,125
Pick something you actually have access to: your own store, a client's, or one

6
00:00:19,125 --> 00:00:22,332
you're studying closely enough to write about honestly.

7
00:00:22,332 --> 00:00:27,171
Everything from the last five lectures gets assembled here into one document you'll

8
00:00:27,171 --> 00:00:29,620
actually use, the same way Dana uses hers.

9
00:00:29,620 --> 00:00:33,702
If nothing comes to mind immediately, don't default to a hypothetical.

10
00:00:33,702 --> 00:00:38,366
Pick a real product from a brand you already buy from, and write the brief as if

11
00:00:38,366 --> 00:00:40,699
you'd been hired to run their next test.

12
00:00:40,699 --> 00:00:45,363
The constraint of a real product, with a real price and a real audience, is what

13
00:00:45,363 --> 00:00:48,045
makes this exercise teach you anything at all.

14
00:00:48,595 --> 00:00:53,586
This is the whole artifact, and you've already built every section of it piece by

15
00:00:53,586 --> 00:00:53,956
piece.

16
00:00:53,956 --> 00:00:57,591
Today's job is assembly, for a product that isn't Harlan's.

17
00:00:57,591 --> 00:01:02,766
Section one is the product truth document from lecture one: price, margin, spec, and

18
00:01:02,766 --> 00:01:03,998
the claims boundary.

19
00:01:03,998 --> 00:01:05,600
Section two is the ladder.

20
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:07,633
Section three is the hook matrix.

21
00:01:07,633 --> 00:01:10,591
Section four is the angle map, twenty rows deep.

22
00:01:10,591 --> 00:01:15,335
Section five is the congruence checklist you'll run before any test launches.

23
00:01:15,335 --> 00:01:20,325
Notice there's nothing here about creative direction, tone, or which generator to

24
00:01:20,325 --> 00:01:20,572
use.

25
00:01:20,572 --> 00:01:21,681
That's deliberate.

26
00:01:21,681 --> 00:01:26,302
This document exists entirely to answer questions before generation starts.

27
00:01:26,302 --> 00:01:30,984
The moment you're deciding what a prompt should sound like, you've left this

28
00:01:30,984 --> 00:01:35,667
document and moved on to Section three, which is exactly where that decision

29
00:01:35,667 --> 00:01:36,160
belongs.

30
00:01:36,160 --> 00:01:41,089
This is also why Dana's copy stays taped to the wall instead of filed away once.

31
00:01:41,089 --> 00:01:45,833
A brief that only gets read on the day it's written isn't actually protecting

32
00:01:45,833 --> 00:01:46,387
anything.

33
00:01:46,387 --> 00:01:51,378
The value is in checking a new idea against it six weeks later, at speed, without

34
00:01:51,378 --> 00:01:54,951
re-litigating the claims boundary from scratch every time.

35
00:01:55,501 --> 00:02:00,329
Here's the order that actually works, and a rough time budget for each step, because

36
00:02:00,329 --> 00:02:04,754
"write a brief" without a budget tends to expand to fill an entire afternoon.

37
00:02:04,754 --> 00:02:09,581
Product truth first, because everything else depends on it, and it should take about

38
00:02:09,581 --> 00:02:13,202
fifteen minutes if you're not overthinking the claims boundary.

39
00:02:13,202 --> 00:02:15,903
Ten minutes each for the ladder and the matrix.

40
00:02:15,903 --> 00:02:20,730
Twenty for the angle map, because that's genuinely the hardest part and deserves the

41
00:02:20,730 --> 00:02:21,017
time.

42
00:02:21,017 --> 00:02:25,845
Five minutes at the end to check your actual landing page against the checklist from

43
00:02:25,845 --> 00:02:26,592
last lecture.

44
00:02:26,592 --> 00:02:31,304
If you're well past the sixty-minute mark and still on the product truth document,

45
00:02:31,304 --> 00:02:32,683
stop and move on anyway.

46
00:02:32,683 --> 00:02:37,511
An imperfect ladder built on a good enough product truth doc beats a perfect product

47
00:02:37,511 --> 00:02:39,924
truth doc with nothing built on top of it.

48
00:02:39,924 --> 00:02:44,579
You can always come back and sharpen the earlier sections once the whole document

49
00:02:44,579 --> 00:02:44,982
exists.

50
00:02:44,982 --> 00:02:48,200
So which section are you actually going to get stuck on?

51
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:53,027
For most people it's the angle map, because twenty genuinely distinct rows is a real

52
00:02:53,027 --> 00:02:54,004
creative stretch.

53
00:02:54,004 --> 00:02:58,544
Knowing that in advance is usually enough to push through it instead of quietly

54
00:02:58,544 --> 00:03:01,303
settling for twelve good rows and eight reskins.

55
00:03:01,853 --> 00:03:06,664
Two shortcuts people take under time pressure, and both cost more than they save.

56
00:03:06,664 --> 00:03:11,415
Skipping the claims boundary because "we'll sort it out later" doesn't avoid the

57
00:03:11,415 --> 00:03:11,712
work.

58
00:03:11,712 --> 00:03:16,702
It just moves it to Section six, where the pre-flight check catches the same problem

59
00:03:16,702 --> 00:03:19,671
after you've already generated variants around it.

60
00:03:19,671 --> 00:03:24,304
And writing mostly for whichever rung is easiest, usually product-aware, feels

61
00:03:24,304 --> 00:03:29,175
productive and quietly leaves four fifths of your funnel with nothing built for it

62
00:03:29,175 --> 00:03:29,591
at all.

63
00:03:29,591 --> 00:03:34,342
Both shortcuts share the same shape: they trade fifteen honest minutes now for a

64
00:03:34,342 --> 00:03:38,916
much more expensive correction later, once real money or real time is already

65
00:03:38,916 --> 00:03:40,401
committed to the mistake.

66
00:03:40,401 --> 00:03:44,915
That trade almost never looks good in hindsight, even though it always feels

67
00:03:44,915 --> 00:03:46,400
reasonable in the moment.

68
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:51,270
Dana holds the claims boundary as hard as she does because her own early ad drafts

69
00:03:51,270 --> 00:03:55,606
made overstatements a compliance team would have rejected in a heartbeat.

70
00:03:55,606 --> 00:03:59,526
Writing the boundary down is what stops that from happening twice.

71
00:04:00,076 --> 00:04:02,710
One honest note before you start the clock.

72
00:04:02,710 --> 00:04:07,855
Done properly, this takes closer to an hour than five minutes, and that's not a flaw

73
00:04:07,855 --> 00:04:08,836
in the exercise.

74
00:04:08,836 --> 00:04:13,552
It's an hour spent once, per product, that every batch, every test, and every

75
00:04:13,552 --> 00:04:16,125
scaling decision downstream gets to reuse.

76
00:04:16,125 --> 00:04:21,025
Measured against a single wasted week of testing angles that were never actually

77
00:04:21,025 --> 00:04:24,578
distinct, it's the cheapest insurance in the whole course.

78
00:04:24,578 --> 00:04:29,234
Block the hour on a calendar rather than promising yourself you'll fit it in

79
00:04:29,234 --> 00:04:29,846
somewhere.

80
00:04:29,846 --> 00:04:34,931
A brief that half-exists in your head is exactly the reskin trap from last lecture,

81
00:04:34,931 --> 00:04:36,401
waiting to happen again.

82
00:04:36,951 --> 00:04:41,701
Once this brief exists, Section three has almost nothing left to decide.

83
00:04:41,701 --> 00:04:44,604
The angle map already says what to generate.

84
00:04:44,604 --> 00:04:49,882
Batch generation just has to generate it, which is exactly the order this course

85
00:04:49,882 --> 00:04:53,181
keeps insisting on: decide first, generate second.

86
00:04:53,181 --> 00:04:56,743
So don't start Section three with an unfinished brief.

87
00:04:56,743 --> 00:05:02,219
Close the five sections on the page now, even roughly, before you open a generator.

88
00:05:02,219 --> 00:05:07,233
That finished page is artifact B01, and it's the only input the next section

89
00:05:07,233 --> 00:05:08,817
actually needs from you.
